        biography: "赵亮 (Liang Zhao) was born in 1992 in Wuhan, Hubei, an industrial city in central China. He grew up in a middle-class family: his father was a mechanical engineer and his mother a mathematics teacher. Since age 12 he was taking apart old computers in the garage of his building, fascinated by understanding how things worked inside.\n\nHe studied Computer Science at Huazhong University in Wuhan, one of the best engineering schools in the country. During university he won a national scholarship for a distributed weather data processing project — his first real experience with systems that scale.\n\nIn 2014, at 22, he moved to Shenzhen with a backpack, a laptop and 3,000 yuan saved. He started as a junior developer at an incubator in the Nanshan district, sleeping in an apartment shared with five other engineers. Ten years later, he is Senior Software Engineer at 汇智云科技股份有限公司 (Huizhiyun Technologies Inc.), leading the company's data architecture from a coworking space in the Nanshan Hi-Tech Park.\n\nLiang is the type who can spend 14 hours debugging a memory leak in Go, but gets completely lost when dealing with non-purely-technical topics. He doesn't care about office politics, hates unnecessary meetings, and his only real weakness is the Fujian oolong tea his mother sends him every month by mail.\n\nHuizhiyun Technologies was in the middle of an expansion process, aiming to build a technological bridge for trade between China and Latin America. The technical challenge was complex: they needed to develop a cross-border multilingual invoicing pipeline. The system required processing invoices that handled multiple simultaneous conversions in a single document, crossing Euros (EUR), US Dollars (USD) and Chinese Yuan (CNY) with real-time exchange rates.\n\nLiang had spent 10 years building distributed systems and microservices architectures. He could make a database process millions of requests per second, but when he sat down to design this system, he hit a wall: he had no idea how the customs, commercial and logistical flows of Latin America worked.\n\nIt was a business domain problem, not a code problem. Liang understood the mathematics of currency conversion, but did not understand the idiosyncrasy of the Latin American market, nor how that invoicing information should be structured so that it really made operational sense for importers on the other side of the world.\n\nSeeking perspective, Liang raised the architectural challenge in an open-source environment (GitHub), seeking to discuss how to structure data pipelines that handled multiple currencies in a single block without losing precision. The title of his query was dry and direct, typical of him: Real-time multi-currency invoice aggregation: CNY/EUR/USD in cross-border pipelines.\n\nSebastián Corona appeared. Sebastián did not respond with a simple code patch. He made a complete analysis: he explained the operational logic behind international purchases in LATAM, how clients needed to visualize those three currencies crossed in the same invoice, and provided solutions on how to process Excel and CSV files with mixed encodings (Western text and Chinese characters) without data corruption. What impressed Liang most was not only the technical skill, but that Sebastián understood the business context that was completely escaping him.\n\nThe technical conversation quickly moved to emails and then to long video calls. Liang, always with his oolong tea next to the laptop, verified that Sebastián possessed the exact piece his team in Shenzhen was missing: a software engineering mind with a native Latin American logistical vision.\n\nShortly after, Liang got straight to the point: Sebastián, at Huizhiyun we are building something that doesn't exist yet: a system that truly connects China with LATAM at the data and invoicing level. I need someone who understands that region better than I do. Are you interested in joining the project and leading this architecture with us?\n\nSebastián accepted the consulting challenge. Together they not only pulled off the complex multilingual invoicing system, but achieved such fluid synergy that they ended up developing additional joint tools, including a preventive malicious file analysis system.\n\nThus a Senior Engineer in Shenzhen ended up integrating a Venezuelan developer into his team. Liang always sums it up with a phrase: Code has no nationality. But business and logistics do.",
